--TEST--
Core\IO::readText throws naming the offset of the first sequence the charset cannot read, with the bound asserted on both sides of the one octet that decides it — the same file read under a charset that can spell it still answers, and `read` never asks, so what the throw is about is the conversion and not the path — spec § 14, `rule:types/conversion`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `cafe\n` is five ASCII octets and reads. One character later the file is
// `café\n`, whose octet at index 3 is 0xC3, and the same call refuses there. A
// member that stopped one octet early, or that reported the start of the buffer
// instead of the start of the sequence, prints a different number below.
Core\IO::write("plain.txt", "cafe\n");
echo Core\IO::readText("plain.txt", {charset: Core\Charset::Ascii});

Core\IO::write("accented.txt", "café\n");
try {
    echo Core\IO::readText("accented.txt", {charset: Core\Charset::Ascii});
    echo "ascii read it\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $notAscii) {
    echo $notAscii->message, "\n";
}

// The same file and the same door, under a charset that can spell it: the
// refusal above was the conversion's, and a file is not made unreadable by
// having been asked the wrong question about it.
echo Core\IO::readText("accented.txt");

// And `read` asks nothing at all, which is the difference between the two
// members stated as an assertion rather than left in a doc comment.
echo Core\IO::read("accented.txt");

Core\IO::remove("plain.txt");
Core\IO::remove("accented.txt");
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
cafe
a conversion is exact or it throws: Core\IO::readText() found at offset 3 a byte sequence that is not `Core\Charset::Ascii`, and nothing is replaced with U+FFFD or dropped
café
café
